From: verkur46 <mohamadrezasahami1999@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 15:52:46 +0330
Subject: [PATCH 016/230] Test that the write endpoints actually return 429
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

writeLimiter was already wired onto both POST /ratings and POST /comments,
so the audit item was stale — but nothing tested it, which meant a
refactor could drop it silently and no one would find out.

The suite drives the real ratingsRouter over real HTTP with a real
session token against a throwaway database: requests 1..60 reach the
handler and get its own 400, request 61 is refused by the limiter with
TOO_MANY_REQUESTS and a sane Retry-After. Asserting the handler's own
status is what makes it a wiring test rather than a unit test — a
limiter mounted after the handler, or on the wrong method, fails it.

Writing it surfaced the real contract: both routes share ONE limiter
instance, so the 60/min budget is shared and alternating endpoints is
not a way around the cap. The test now pins that instead of assuming
per-route budgets.
